    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the main difference between a generator and an inverter generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A regular generator spins its alternator at a fixed 3,600 RPM to make 60 hertz power directly. An inverter generator converts its alternator output to DC and then back to clean AC electronically, so the engine can slow down at light loads. That makes inverters quieter, more fuel efficient and cleaner, but more expensive per watt."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is an inverter generator better than a regular generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is better for camping, quiet running, light loads and sensitive electronics. A regular generator is better value when you need high wattage, 240 volts or a 50-amp outlet for home backup. Neither is better across the board; the right choice depends on how many watts you need and where the generator will run."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a power inverter generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is another name for an inverter generator: a fuel-burning generator that uses a rectifier and inverter to produce its AC output, so engine speed is not tied to frequency. It is different from a standalone power inverter, which converts battery DC to AC, and from a portable power station, which has no engine at all."
          }
        },
