        <div id="how-they-make-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Inverter Generator vs Generator: How Each One Makes AC
          </h2>

          <p>
            A regular generator, the kind sold as an open-frame or conventional portable, bolts an alternator straight to the engine crankshaft. Because the alternator's output frequency is tied directly to how fast it spins, a two-pole alternator has to turn at 3,600 RPM to make 60 hertz power. The governor holds the engine at that speed whether you are running one work light or a well pump. That fixed speed is the root of nearly every difference covered on this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inverter generator, sometimes searched as a power inverter generator, does it in three stages. The engine spins a multi-pole alternator that produces high-frequency, wild AC. A rectifier turns that into DC, and an inverter module then chops the DC back into a clean 120-volt, 60-hertz sine wave using fast switching transistors under microprocessor control. Because the output frequency is built electronically, the engine is free to change speed with demand.
          </p>

          <p>
            That answers the question of what a power inverter generator is: a generator whose engine speed and output frequency are decoupled by electronics. It is not the same as a battery-fed power inverter that plugs into a car, and it is not a power station. It burns gasoline or propane like any other generator, it just does not care how fast it is spinning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our explainer on <a href="/how-do-portable-generators-work/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how portable generators work</a> covers the alternator, governor and AVR in detail, so this page does not repeat it. What matters for a buying decision is the consequence: one design is locked at full speed, the other throttles down, and the electronics that let it throttle are what you pay for, what make it quiet, and what you would eventually have to replace.
          </p>

