        <div id="clean-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Clean Power: THD and Sensitive Electronics
          </h2>

          <p>
            Total harmonic distortion, written THD, measures how far a generator's waveform strays from a perfect sine wave. Utility power usually sits in the low single digits. Inverter generators are typically rated under 3 percent, and many spec sheets list under 1.5 percent at rated load. Conventional generators vary much more widely. Budget open-frame units are often quoted somewhere between 10 and 25 percent, while some newer conventional models with better alternator windings advertise under 5 or 6 percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why does that matter? Devices with switch-mode power supplies, which covers laptops, phone chargers and most modern TVs, are usually tolerant because they rectify the input to DC anyway. The equipment that complains is the stuff that relies on a clean wave or reads it for timing: furnace and boiler control boards, variable-speed motors, some refrigerators with inverter compressors, medical devices like CPAP machines with heated humidifiers, UPS units that reject dirty input and click between modes, and audio gear that hums.
          </p>

          <p>
            The waveform shape is the same idea covered in our guide to <a href="/pure-sine-wave-vs-modified-sine-wave/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pure sine wave versus modified sine wave</a>, though a conventional generator does not produce a stepped modified wave. It produces a rough sine wave with harmonics riding on it, and its voltage and frequency also wander when a big motor kicks on. That voltage sag during motor starts is often more of a problem for electronics than the steady-state THD figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule: if a gas furnace, a sump pump controller, a home office or anything medical is on your outage list, either buy an inverter or buy a conventional model whose spec sheet states a THD under 5 percent. A plain spec sheet with no THD figure at all usually means the number is not flattering. Our page on whether <a href="/can-a-generator-power-a-furnace-refrigerator-and-sump-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">a generator can power a furnace, refrigerator and sump pump</a> explains why furnace boards in particular are picky.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No THD figure on the spec sheet? Assume it is high.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Manufacturers who have clean output publish it. If you need clean power and the listing is silent, either choose a different model or plan on running sensitive loads from a battery or UPS instead.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-and-fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Noise and Fuel Economy at Partial Load
          </h2>

          <p>
            Noise is where the difference is most obvious to anyone standing nearby. Because a conventional generator holds 3,600 RPM all the time, it is close to as loud running a phone charger as running a space heater. Typical open-frame units are rated around 68 to 76 dBA at 23 feet. Inverter generators in the 2,000-watt class are commonly rated around 48 to 58 dBA at quarter load, and their closed shells and mufflers are designed around that number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Decibels are logarithmic, so the gap is bigger than it sounds. A 10 dBA drop is generally perceived as about half as loud. Going from 72 dBA to 55 dBA is the difference between raising your voice to talk next to the machine and holding a normal conversation. That is why most campgrounds and many national parks set quiet-hour limits that only inverter generators can realistically meet, and why it is the default choice in our <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best generator for camping</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel economy follows the same logic. Most generators spend most of their lives at partial load: a fridge cycling, a few lights, a router, a furnace blower. A conventional engine at 3,600 RPM burns a significant amount of fuel just to keep itself spinning at speed, whatever the load. An inverter in eco mode drops its RPM to match demand, and at a quarter load owners commonly report fuel savings in the range of 20 to 40 percent compared with a similar-sized conventional unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spec sheets show it clearly. The Honda EU2200ITAN, for example, is rated for up to 8.1 hours at quarter load on a 0.95-gallon tank. A typical 3,500-watt open-frame unit with a 4-gallon tank might list 10 to 12 hours at a quarter load, which sounds better until you notice it burns roughly four times the fuel to do it. During a multi-day outage, when gas stations may be closed or have lines, that fuel efficiency matters as much as the wattage rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that eco mode helps least when you need it most. Run an inverter near its rated output and its engine is at full speed, just as loud and nearly as thirsty as a conventional machine. Eco mode also slows the response to sudden loads, so owners often switch it off before starting a well pump or air conditioner to avoid a stall or an overload trip.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Ratings, Not Measurements</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3,600</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">RPM a regular generator must hold</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~10 dBA</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Drop perceived as half as loud</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-40%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fuel saved in eco mode, owners report</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]"><3%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical inverter THD rating</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-and-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cost Per Watt and Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the conventional generator's best argument, and it is a strong one. Open-frame generators in the 3,500 to 5,000-watt range commonly sell for roughly 10 to 20 cents per running watt at street prices. Inverter generators typically cost two to three times as much per running watt, and premium brands more than that. A name-brand 2,000-watt inverter can cost about as much as a big-box 8,000-watt conventional unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put another way, the same money buys a different job. A budget around $600 to $800 gets you either a quality 2,000 to 3,000-watt inverter that runs a fridge, lights, internet and phones quietly, or a conventional generator in the <a href="/best-3500-watt-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">3,500-watt</a> to 5,000-watt class that adds a window air conditioner, a microwave or a well pump. Neither is wrong. They answer different questions about what has to keep running.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight cuts the other way. Inverter generators are built around lighter multi-pole alternators and plastic housings, so the 2,000-watt class typically weighs 40 to 50 pounds and can be carried with one hand. A conventional 3,500-watt unit commonly weighs 100 to 130 pounds and needs a wheel kit. Above about 5,000 watts the gap closes: large inverters weigh 150 to 260 pounds, and the Honda EU7000ISNAN is listed at about 261 pounds dry, heavier than many conventional machines of similar output.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest cost comparison depends on size. Under about 3,000 watts, an inverter costs more but is so much lighter, quieter and more frugal that most buyers who only need that much power are better served by one. Above about 5,000 watts, the inverter premium is large and its portability advantage mostly disappears, so conventional and open-frame inverter hybrids win on value for home backup.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Size class</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Regular generator</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Inverter generator</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">About 2,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Few worth buying</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40-50 lb, best value for light loads</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,500-4,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-130 lb, cheapest per watt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually open-frame inverters, priced higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">7,000-12,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Widely available with 240V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Few models, very expensive</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="240v-and-parallel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 240V, 50-Amp Outlets and Parallel Kits
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you want to back up a house through a transfer switch or interlock, voltage matters more than waveform. Well pumps, many central-air condensers, electric dryers and some furnace setups run on 240 volts, and most transfer switches are fed from a 120/240V twist-lock outlet such as an L14-30 or a 14-50. Most conventional generators from about 5,000 watts up have that outlet. Most inverter generators under about 4,000 watts do not; they are 120-volt only.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inverter models with 240 volts do exist, but they cluster at the top of the range. The Honda EU7000ISNAN, for example, is rated at 5,500 running watts with 120/240V output, and several brands now sell larger open-frame inverters with 240V twist-lock outlets. If you need a 50-amp outlet for a whole-panel interlock or a big RV, the selection is still overwhelmingly conventional, which is why our <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best generator with a 50-amp outlet</a> list leans that way. Our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">transfer switch guide</a> covers the hookup side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Parallel kits are the inverter's answer. Two compatible inverters joined by a parallel cable act as one larger supply, so two 2,200-watt units give you roughly 3,600 running watts combined. Most parallel kits add a 30-amp TT-30R outlet for RVs, which is 120 volts only. That is the key limitation: paralleling small inverters adds wattage, but in most cases it does not give you 240 volts.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a camper, parallel capability is excellent. You carry two 47-pound boxes instead of one 120-pound one, run a single unit overnight for the fridge and fans, and link both when the air conditioner needs to start. For a homeowner who wants to feed a 240V well pump, parallel kits are usually the wrong path, and a single conventional or 240V-capable open-frame inverter is simpler.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CHECK THIS FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Parallel kits add watts, usually not volts.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Two linked 120V inverters still give you 120V. If anything on your list runs on 240V, look for a generator with a 120/240V twist-lock or 14-50 outlet on the panel itself.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="reliability-and-repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Reliability and Repair Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both types share the same basic engine parts: carburetor or EFI, spark plug, air filter, recoil. The most common failure on either one is a gummed-up carburetor from stale fuel, and that costs the same to fix on both. Where they differ is in the electrical side, and it is worth knowing what a failure costs before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A conventional generator's electrical system is simple. The usual failures are worn brushes, a failed automatic voltage regulator, or on brushless designs a capacitor. Those parts commonly cost tens of dollars and can often be swapped with basic tools by anyone comfortable with a multimeter. A failed alternator winding is the expensive outcome, and on a budget machine it often means the whole unit is written off.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inverter generator adds an inverter module and control board. When those fail, from a lightning surge, overheating in a badly ventilated spot, or simple component age, replacement modules commonly cost several hundred dollars and may be dealer-only parts for some brands. On a cheap inverter that can approach the cost of a new unit. Premium brands have better parts availability and service networks, which is a real part of what their higher price buys.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither design is inherently fragile. Well-built inverters from established brands routinely last many years of outage and camping use. The point is that a no-name inverter is a riskier purchase than a no-name conventional generator, because when the conventional one fails it is usually a cheap, generic fix. If you are buying on price, a reputable conventional model is often the safer bet; if you are buying an inverter, buy a brand with parts support.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is otherwise identical: change the oil on the manufacturer's schedule, run the engine monthly or drain the fuel for storage, use stabiliser, and keep it dry. Inverters do benefit from extra care about ventilation, because their electronics live inside an enclosure and heat is their enemy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy-which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Generator vs Inverter Generator: Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy an inverter generator if you camp, tailgate or RV, if you will run it within earshot of neighbours, if your outage load is mostly a fridge, lights, internet and electronics, or if a furnace control board, CPAP or home office is on the list. A 2,000 to 3,500-watt inverter covers that work quietly and frugally, and for many households it is the best first generator. Our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best inverter generator</a> guide ranks the models worth that premium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy a regular generator if your priority is running a well pump, central air, an electric range or a whole panel through an interlock, and the generator will sit outside, at least 20 feet from the house, during outages. You get 240 volts, 30 or 50-amp outlets and the most watts per dollar. For large homes that means the <a href="/best-10000-watt-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">10,000-watt class</a> or above, where inverter equivalents are scarce and costly. If clean power still matters, look for one with a published THD under 5 percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            A hybrid route works for a lot of people: a big conventional generator for heavy loads, plus a small inverter or a battery for overnight quiet running and sensitive electronics. Some owners pair a conventional generator with a power station that charges from it, and run electronics from the battery. Our <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">power station versus generator</a> comparison covers that approach, and if heating is the worry, see whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-gas-furnace/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">a portable power station can run a gas furnace</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still undecided, list everything you want running at once, add up running watts and the largest single surge using our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">generator wattage chart</a>, and note anything that needs 240 volts. If the total is under about 3,500 running watts and nothing needs 240V, buy an inverter. If the total is higher or you need 240V, buy a conventional generator, and spend the savings on a proper transfer switch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming parallel kits give you 240V</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Linking two small inverters adds wattage, but most parallel kits only provide 120V outlets and a TT-30R RV socket. If your well pump or transfer switch needs 240V, two paralleled 2,000-watt units will not run it. Check the parallel kit's outlets before counting on it for home backup.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving eco mode on for motor starts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Eco mode lets the engine idle down, which means it reacts more slowly when a big motor kicks on. Owners commonly report stalls or overload trips when a fridge compressor or pump starts in eco mode. Switch it off before starting heavy loads, then turn it back on once they are running.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing starting watts instead of running watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Listings lead with the bigger starting or peak figure. An inverter sold as 2,200 watts is typically rated for 1,800 running watts. Compare running watts between models, because that is what the machine can sustain for hours with your fridge, furnace and lights all on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a conventional unit with no THD rating for a furnace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Furnace control boards, variable-speed motors and some UPS units dislike dirty power. A budget open-frame generator with no published THD figure may run them, or may cause lockouts and nuisance faults. If those loads matter, buy an inverter or a conventional model rated under 5 percent THD.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying the inverter premium for a driveway-only machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the generator will only ever sit outside during outages, feeding a transfer switch and heavy loads, the quietness and light weight you pay double for may not matter. A quality conventional unit with low published THD can deliver more usable watts for the same money.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the main difference between a generator and an inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A regular generator spins its alternator at a fixed 3,600 RPM to make 60 hertz power directly. An inverter generator converts its alternator output to DC and then back to clean AC electronically, so the engine can slow down at light loads. That makes inverters quieter, more fuel efficient and cleaner, but more expensive per watt.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an inverter generator better than a regular generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is better for camping, quiet running, light loads and sensitive electronics. A regular generator is better value when you need high wattage, 240 volts or a 50-amp outlet for home backup. Neither is better across the board; the right choice depends on how many watts you need and where the generator will run.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a power inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is another name for an inverter generator: a fuel-burning generator that uses a rectifier and inverter to produce its AC output, so engine speed is not tied to frequency. It is different from a standalone power inverter, which converts battery DC to AC, and from a portable power station, which has no engine at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a regular generator damage electronics?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can stress them. Budget conventional generators often have high THD and voltage swings when motors start, which can upset furnace boards, UPS units and some variable-speed appliances. Most phone chargers and laptops cope fine. Using a conventional model rated under 5 percent THD, or running electronics through a battery or UPS, reduces the risk considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do inverter generators use less fuel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At light and partial loads, yes. Eco mode lets the engine slow down to match demand, and owners commonly report fuel savings of 20 to 40 percent versus a similar conventional unit at quarter load. At or near full load the difference largely disappears, because the inverter's engine has to run at full speed too.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an inverter generator power a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Small ones cannot, and most are 120 volts only, so they cannot feed 240V circuits through a transfer switch. Larger inverters such as the 120/240V Honda EU7000ISNAN can run essential circuits, but they cost far more than conventional generators of the same output. For whole-house backup, a conventional generator is usually the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are inverter generators more expensive to repair?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The engine repairs cost the same. The difference is the inverter module and control board, which commonly cost several hundred dollars to replace and may only be available from a dealer. Conventional generators usually fail in cheaper parts like brushes, voltage regulators or capacitors. Buying an inverter from a brand with good parts support reduces that risk.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

