        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            An inverter generator is the better machine for quiet running, light loads and sensitive electronics. A regular generator is the better deal for raw watts, 240 volts and whole-panel backup. The waveform gap has narrowed as good conventional models publish low THD figures; the price-per-watt gap has not.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Add up your running watts, check whether anything needs 240V, and decide where the generator will sit. Under 3,500 watts with no 240V loads, buy an inverter. Above that, or with a well pump or transfer switch in the plan, buy a conventional generator and spend the difference on doing the hookup properly.
          </p>
